This product includes software developed by third parties. The obligation this
file exists to satisfy is a real one and easy to overlook: a Vita `.vpk` built
with this toolchain **statically links** the Flutter engine, Skia, the Dart
runtime, ICU, HarfBuzz and their dependencies into `eboot.bin`. That makes the
`.vpk` a binary distribution of BSD-3-licensed software, and clause 2 of BSD-3
requires the copyright notice and disclaimer to travel with it — in "the
documentation and/or other materials provided with the distribution".

Shipping a `.vpk` without those materials is a licence violation regardless of
whether this repository is public.

## What to ship alongside a .vpk

`LICENSE.engine`, from the engine artifact
(`flutter-engine-vita-<version>/LICENSE.engine`).

That file is Flutter's own generated aggregate of every third-party licence in
the engine — around 30,000 lines covering Skia, Dart, ICU, HarfBuzz, libpng,
zlib, FreeType, Abseil and the rest. It is generated by upstream's licence
tooling from the engine sources, which is why it is shipped rather than
hand-written here: an inventory assembled from memory would be both incomplete
and unverifiable, and this one is neither.

It is taken from the Flutter SDK at the pinned revision — the same sources this
project builds — so it describes the components actually linked. The only delta
is this project's own patches, which are covered by [LICENSE](LICENSE).

## vitaGL and vitaShaRK are LGPL, not BSD

Everything above is BSD-3. Two components are not, and they are linked into
every `eboot.bin` this toolchain produces, software-path apps included.

| Component | Licence |
|---|---|
| [vitaGL](https://github.com/Rinnegatamante/vitaGL) | LGPL-3.0-or-later |
| [vitaShaRK](https://github.com/Rinnegatamante/vitaShaRK) | LGPL-3.0-or-later |

vitaGL gives us GLES2 over `sceGxm` and vitaShaRK compiles shaders through
Sony's `libshacccg`. Both are Rinnegatamante's, both ship as static archives,
and `templates/vita-app/CMakeLists.txt` links them unconditionally.

Section 4 of LGPL-3 allows a combined work with a statically linked library on
one further condition: whoever conveys the binary must also supply what a
recipient needs to relink it against a modified version of that library. Object
files and the link line satisfy that. So does a shared library mechanism, under
section 4(d)(1), which is why the module route exists in
[ADR-0018](docs/adr/0018-the-lgpl-obligation-in-every-vpk.md).

**This applies to you if you ship a `.vpk` built with this toolchain.** It is
not a duty this project can discharge on your behalf, because you are the one
conveying the binary. Alongside `LICENSE.engine`, ship vitaGL's `COPYING` and
`COPYING.LESSER`, the vitaGL and vitaShaRK sources at the revisions in
[VERSIONS.md](VERSIONS.md), the patch series in [patches/vitagl/](patches/vitagl/),
and your application's object files.

ADR-0018 is measuring whether this stays true. Read it before you build a
release pipeline around the paragraph above.

## The patch series is not all BSD-3

A patch is a derivative work of the file it changes, so each series under
[patches/](patches/) carries the licence of the project it patches, not this
project's. The [LICENSE](LICENSE) at the root covers this repository's own code
and does not reach them.

| Series | Licence it inherits |
|---|---|
| `patches/dart-sdk/`, `patches/flutter-engine/`, `patches/skia/` | BSD-3-Clause |
| `patches/vitagl/` | LGPL-3.0-or-later |
| `patches/abseil/` | Apache-2.0 |
| `patches/boringssl/` | see BoringSSL's own `LICENSE` |
| `patches/test-apps/` | whatever each application uses; they are unrelated projects |

